    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "##  🔍Teacher Notes\r\nThis activity is from <a href='https://studio.code.org/s/aif1-2025/lessons/3' target='_blank' rel='noopener noreferrer' style='text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;'>Lesson 3</a>. We've modified this activity for PL in two ways:\r\n1. Combined three separate curriculum levels into one.\r\n2. Turned partner discussion into individual reflections.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Minimal Context Given\r\n\r\nPractice prompting through the career of a video game developer by giving a simple, unstructured prompt to see how AI creates dialogue without any examples or guidance.\r\n\r\n### Do This:\r\n\r\n1. Try a basic prompt like the one below, or a similar one related to character conversations.\r\n\r\n>Write a conversation between a superhero and their sidekick.\r\n\r\n2. Press **Send**.\r\n3. Read the response you get, paying attention to how interesting, creative, or repetitive the dialogue is.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Providing a Single Example\r\n\r\nPractice prompting through the career of a video game developer by adding a single example to help guide the AI's dialogue.\r\n\r\n### Do This:\r\n\r\n1. Decide on **a brief example** of a hero-sidekick exchange to include in your prompt.\r\n2. Prompt AI to generate a conversation by including your example.\r\n2. Press **Send**.\r\n3. Read the response you get, noticing how the AI uses the example to shape tone, style, or plot.\r\n\r\n::: details [**Not sure where to start?**]\r\nHere's an example prompt:\r\n> Write a very short dialogue between a superhero and their sidekick where the sidekick is lovable but foolish. For example:\r\n>\r\n> Hero: \"I told you to stay in the jet!\"\r\n>\r\n> Sidekick: \"And miss this explosion? Never.\"\r\n:::\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Providing Multiple Examples\r\n\r\nPractice prompting through the career of a video game developer by guiding the AI with clear structure and multiple expectations for the dialogue.\r\n\r\n### Do This:\r\n\r\n1. Prompt AI to generate a conversation by including **multiple examples** of a hero-sidekick exchange.\r\n2. Before sending it, think about the difference between your prompt on this level and the previous one.\r\n2. Press **Send**.\r\n3. Read the response you get, paying attention to how the added structure affects the story flow, character depth, and tension.\r\n\r\n::: details [**Not sure where to start?**]\r\nHere's an example prompt:\r\n> Write a very short dialogue between a superhero and their sidekick where the sidekick is lovable but foolish. For example:\r\n>\r\n> Hero: \"I told you to stay in the jet!\"\r\n>\r\n> Sidekick: \"And miss this explosion? Never.\"\r\n>\r\n> OR\r\n>\r\n> Hero: \"We're running out of time—hand me the anti-gravity boots!\"\r\n>\r\n> Sidekick: \"Uh... you said we’d never need them again, so I traded them for tacos.\"\r\n>\r\n> Hero: \"...Worth it?\"\r\n>\r\n> Sidekick [*mouth full*]:  \"So worth it.\"\r\n>\r\n> OR\r\n>\r\n> Hero: \"This mission requires silence, precision, and zero distractions.\"\r\n>\r\n> Sidekick [*throws something behind him*]: \"I guess I won't be needing this bagpipe.\"\r\n>\r\n:::\r\n",
